# TODO

Updated on 2026-10-30 based on source review, tests, and build checks. This is
an implementation backlog, not confirmation of full RFC 6733 compliance.
All paths below are relative to the repository root.

## Already implemented

- `include/diameter/message/`: Header, Message, and AVP structures, flags,
  basic and derived value types, Grouped values, size and padding calculation.
- `include/diameter/serial/`: encoding and decoding through Netpacker,
  decoded values stored as OctetString with subsequent type conversion;
  checks for the protocol version and minimum header and AVP lengths.
- `include/diameter/application/common/`: base command, AVP, and Result-Code
  constants; builders for CER/CEA, DWR/DWA, and DPR/DPA. There is no general
  command validator; individual Diameter applications are not implemented yet.
- `include/diameter/core/io/`: asynchronous TCP accept/connect, message reads
  using the header and declared length, and a write queue. SCTP and TLS/DTLS
  are not implemented.
- `include/diameter/core/peer/peer.h`, `src/core/peer/peer.cpp`: the Peer state
  table, outgoing and incoming connection handling, election, control messages,
  and callbacks. The main branches exist but need fixes and tests.
- `include/diameter/core/manager/manager.h`: object creation from configuration,
  matching incoming CER messages to configured peers, and control request
  generation. The public application message exchange API is incomplete.
- `include/diameter/core/controller/`: waiting for the first CER and tracking
  outgoing requests with timers. WatchdogController consists mostly of stubs
  and is not connected to Manager.
- `include/diameter/log/`: a logging macro contract and an example logger_impl.h.

## Initial review checks (before the unit test expansion)

- `cmake --build build -j 2` and `ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure`
  pass in the existing Debug configuration, including the example builds.
  The sources contained 64 test cases, 7 of which were empty; the Peer and Manager
  tests are empty. Passing CTest therefore does not establish that the network
  core works correctly.
- Small standalone programs confirmed the following cases:
  a Message declaring a length of 28 consumes 32 bytes when an AVP extends past
  its boundary; an AVP with Vendor-Id present and V cleared has size()=16 but
  encodes to 12 bytes; make_peer_info() accepts a CER missing mandatory AVPs;
  a cancelled timer from an old request completes a new request with the same
  IDs with a Timeout error.
- A clean configuration in `/tmp` with `DIAMETER_HEADER_ONLY=ON`, tests, and
  examples fails to link: definitions of the Peer tables and make_peer_info()
  are missing. Boost 1.84 was located using CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH from the existing
  build configuration.
- TLS, SCTP, and interoperability with an external Diameter implementation
  were not tested.

## Unit test expansion — 2026-10-30

- Added 138 executable test cases, including template instantiations, and filled
  all 5 Serial placeholders. The suite now runs 202 cases: 169 pass and 33 fail
  with 67 failed assertions. The 2 empty Peer/Manager tests remain outside this
  Message/Serial-focused change. Production code was not modified.
- The build succeeds. CTest intentionally returns failure while the exposed
  defects remain. Regressions are active tests, without expected-failure markers
  or disabled cases. Existing header fixtures now use Version=1 and valid
  Request/Proxiable/Retransmitted flags rather than Request+Error.
- Coverage includes exact wire bytes and iterator positions, every truncated
  header/frame/output range for small fixtures, AVP padding, Grouped nesting,
  numeric limits and IEEE values, value conversions, copy/move ownership,
  derived value validation, and 2036/2104 Time boundaries.
- Latest local outputs: `build/tests/unit-test.log` (CTest output) and
  `build/tests/unit-report.xml` (per-case Boost.Test report). These are generated
  files in the ignored build directory, not repository artifacts.
- An isolated Serial run under ASan/UBSan executes 118 cases: 102 pass and
  the same 16 Serial regressions fail, with no sanitizer diagnostics.
  Leak detection was disabled; this is not a leak-check result.

Reproduce all cases with `ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure`.
Run an individual case with, for example:

```sh
build/tests/tests --run_test=serial_message_boundaries/decode_does_not_use_following_frame_to_finish_an_avp --log_level=test_suite
```

Failing regression groups and corresponding work items:

| Area | Test suite/cases | Required fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Message boundaries | `serial_message_boundaries/decode_rejects_*`, `decode_does_not_use_following_frame_to_finish_an_avp` | Reject AVPs/partial headers/padding crossing the declared frame boundary without consuming the next frame. |
| Cached length | `serial_message_boundaries/encode_does_not_emit_zero_or_stale_message_length`, `encode_detects_length_stale_after_avp_mutation` | Normalize the length or reject the inconsistent Message before sending it. |
| Header validation | `serial_header_boundaries/decode_rejects_unaligned_message_lengths`, `encode_rejects_*` | Reject invalid versions/lengths and 24-bit field overflow. |
| AVP invariants | `avp/decode_rejects_zero_vendor_id`, `encode_rejects_zero_vendor_id`, `encode_rejects_vendor_id_without_vendor_flag`, `encode_rejects_avp_length_that_does_not_fit_24_bits` | Validate the optional header and reject length truncation. |
| Padding | `avp/encode_initializes_all_padding_bytes` | Write zero padding regardless of existing buffer contents. |
| Const access | `basic_avp_value_tests/const_get_accessor_preserves_constness` | Correct the const get() signature/body; the test inspects the signature so the suite still compiles. |
| Text addresses | `avp_address/text_address_rejects_embedded_nul_suffix` | Reject IPv4/IPv6 strings with data after an embedded NUL. |
| IDNA | `avp_diameter_identity/internationalized_names_accept_ascii_a_labels`, `avp_diameter_uri/internationalized_host_accepts_ascii_a_labels` | Accept ASCII A-labels in the final DNS label. |
| URI validation | `avp_diameter_uri/udp_*`, `port_larger_than_sixteen_bits_is_rejected` | Reject UDP for explicit/default Diameter and out-of-range ports. |
| Filter grammar | `avp_ip_filter_rule/*` | Validate IPs/masks/ports and support numeric protocol, assigned/negated addresses, port lists, and established options. |
| Time conversion | `avp_time/pre_unix_epoch_time_can_be_represented` | Support representable pre-1970 Unix timestamps when converting to/from NTP era zero. |

## P0 — fix before using the network core

- [ ] Complete the public Manager API: peer lookup/access, sending requests
  with response callbacks and timeouts, sending answers, and send failure
  notifications. on_recv_message_handler() currently ignores message;
  registered user callbacks for messages and state changes are never called.
  Align IPeer with Peer: Peer does not inherit IPeer, although Manager exposes
  it in callback signatures. Check: application request/answer exchange through
  Manager. Files: `core/manager/manager.h`, `core/peer/{ipeer,peer}.h` under
  include/diameter.

- [ ] Fix Connector error propagation to Peer — an original TODO item.
  After a resolve/bind/connect attempt is exhausted, call on_connect_cb(error)
  so that I_RCV_CONN_NACK is raised. Assign the reconnect policy to a single
  layer and eliminate indefinite internal retries that hide the result of
  an attempt from the FSM. File: `include/diameter/core/io/connector.h`.

- [ ] Implement Connector::stop() — an original TODO item: cancel the resolver,
  timer, and connect operation, close any socket not yet handed off, release
  callbacks, and call on_stop_cb once. Currently, only two atomic flags change.
  Define the contract for calling run() again after stop()/success and check
  cancellation at every connection stage.
  File: `include/diameter/core/io/connector.h`.

- [ ] Bound Message decoding by its own declared length, check the remaining
  space before reading each AVP and subtracting avp.size(), and require exact
  consumption of the body. Apply the same fix to Grouped parsing, which also
  subtracts sizes without checking. The current code can consume data from
  the next message and cause unsigned underflow. Checks: truncated data, an AVP
  larger than the remaining body, multiple messages in one buffer, and malformed
  nested Grouped values. Files: `include/diameter/serial/message.h`,
  `include/diameter/serial/avp/avp.h`.

- [ ] Keep V and the optional Vendor-Id consistent when calculating lengths
  and serializing: length() uses the presence of vendor_id, whereas put() uses
  the V flag. Reject both inconsistent combinations before writing. Check
  Vendor-Id=0 with V=1. Files: `include/diameter/message/avp/avp.h`,
  `include/diameter/serial/avp/avp.h`.

- [ ] Protect MessageController against late callbacks from cancelled timers:
  check operation_aborted before modifying the table and bind the handler to
  a specific request/timer instance. Currently, looking up only the peer and
  IDs allows an old timer to remove a new request. Checks: answer/timeout,
  disconnect/timeout, and ID reuse; exactly one completion per request.
  File: `include/diameter/core/controller/message_controller.h`.

- [ ] Eliminate recursive lock acquisition and calls to external code while
  holding mutexes. For example, configure() holds m_mutex and calls peer->stop();
  when the peer is open, DPR generation acquires m_mutex again. Incoming CER
  handling also invokes IncomingController and Peer callbacks under locks.
  Copy the callback/state under the lock and invoke it after releasing the lock;
  check reentry into the library API from user callbacks.
  Files: Manager, Peer, IncomingController.

- [ ] Add explicit Manager shutdown and safe controller cleanup: cancel
  operations, complete pending requests, and prevent handlers capturing `this`
  from accessing a destroyed owner. Break shared_ptr cycles between Peer,
  Connector/Connection, and callbacks. Checks: peer removal, Manager destruction
  with active I/O and timers, and absence of leaks and use-after-free.
  Files: `include/diameter/core/{manager,controller,peer,io}/*.h`.

- [ ] Validate CER/CEA before successfully opening Peer: mandatory AVPs,
  the answer Result-Code, the expected remote identity, and application
  compatibility. Manager currently always builds a successful CEA,
  process_CEA() only parses PeerInfo, and receiving CEA in WAIT_RETURNS bypasses
  process_CEA() entirely. Checks: a negative/invalid CEA does not move the peer
  to OPEN; invalid AVP values do not let exceptions escape an Asio handler.
  Files: Manager, Peer, `src/core/peer/info.cpp`.

- [ ] Do not ignore use_tls=true: until a secure transport is implemented,
  explicitly reject this configuration instead of creating a plain TCP
  connection. Complete TLS support separately under P1 below.
  Files: `include/diameter/core/config/config.h`, Manager, I/O.

## P1 — complete the base protocol and fix partial implementations

### Message and serial

- [ ] Validate 24-bit fields when writing: Message/AVP lengths and Command-Code,
  total size overflow, and Message Length alignment to four bytes. Validate
  lengths before allocating the receive buffer. The header currently checks
  only the minimum length of 20, and writing three bytes can truncate a value.
  Files: `message/{message.h,header/*,avp/avp.h}`,
  `serial/{header/header.h,avp/avp.h}` under include/diameter.

- [ ] Write zero AVP padding explicitly: skipbytes() only advances the iterator,
  so the result depends on the previous contents of the caller's buffer.
  Check: serialization into a buffer prefilled with nonzero bytes.
  File: `include/diameter/serial/avp/avp.h`.

- [ ] Remove the requirement to call size() before serialization:
  Message::size() updates header.length, while put(Message) writes it as is.
  Define a consistent length calculation/validation contract and provide safe
  default values for Header, AVP, BasicValue, and PeerInfo::vendor_id.
  Checks: builder -> encode directly, and encoding again after modifying AVPs.

- [ ] Validate header semantics and command/AVP flags, including R/E/T/P
  combinations, reserved bit handling on receive and send, and command/application
  IDs. Separate structural decoding from command-specific validation; preserving
  an unknown AVP as OctetString is valid in itself.
  Files: `include/diameter/serial/`, `include/diameter/application/common/`.

### Peer, control commands, and capabilities

- [ ] Extend make_peer_info() to check AVP presence and occurrence counts,
  using explicit counters instead of zero/empty strings as absence indicators.
  Account for the AVP Vendor-Id when identifying base AVPs; parse Host-IP-Address,
  Result-Code, Origin-State-Id, and Firmware-Revision. Validate occurrence counts
  of nested AVPs in Vendor-Specific-Application-Id.
  Files: `src/core/peer/info.cpp`, `include/diameter/core/peer/info.h`.

- [ ] Preserve the distinction between vendor-specific Auth-Application-Id and
  Acct-Application-Id: PeerInfo currently stores both in one
  set<pair<vendor,application>>, and Manager generates all of them as Auth.
  Retain remote capabilities and the negotiated application set, and use them
  when sending. Check relay support and NO_COMMON_APPLICATION; verify application
  matching against RFC §5.3 and §6.11.
  Files: PeerInfo, make_peer_info(), Manager, Peer.

- [ ] Implement a common mechanism for building protocol-error answers and
  error CEAs: Result-Code, Error-Message, Failed-AVP, the required flags, and
  identifiers from the original request. Do not send error answers in response
  to answers. Error code enums and exceptions exist, but their mapping to
  protocol responses is incomplete. Files: `application/common/message_builder.h`,
  `core/error.h`, `serial/error.h`, Manager, and Peer under include/diameter.

- [ ] Add validators for CER/CEA, DWR/DWA, and DPR/DPA, and error builders:
  AVP requirements, occurrence counts, types, allowed values, and unknown
  mandatory AVPs, including nested Grouped values. Check DWA/DPA Result-Code
  and DPR Disconnect-Cause. Builders currently allow arbitrary incomplete
  messages, and adding Result-Code implicitly changes Request to Answer.
  Files: `include/diameter/application/common/`, Peer.

- [ ] Fix dispatch of messages with Application-Id=0: unknown commands are
  currently silently dropped in default branches, including cases that should
  raise I_Rcv-Non-CEA in WAIT_CEA. Deliver valid messages to application logic
  or produce the appropriate error; report unexpected events.
  File: `include/diameter/core/peer/peer.h`.

- [ ] Check every FSM branch against the table in RFC §5.6, including both
  orderings of simultaneous connect/CER, election loss, disconnection of either
  connection in ELECT/WAIT_RETURNS, and handling of an error CEA. Add local
  START/handshake cancellation: STOP is currently absent in intermediate states.
  Preserve the intended transition/enter action order: actions read the old
  state, so do not change StateMachine mechanically.
  Files: `include/diameter/core/fsm.h`, Peer, `src/core/peer/peer.cpp`.

- [ ] Complete DPR/DPA shutdown: wait for DPA after a local DPR, send DPA before
  closing after an incoming DPR, enforce a CLOSING timeout, and release all
  resources. Incoming DPR currently does not start a close timer; without
  disconnection by the other side, the peer can remain in CLOSING. Account for
  Disconnect-Cause when reconnecting instead of always using REBOOTING.
  Files: Peer, Manager, MessageController.

- [ ] Use consistent ASCII case-insensitive DiameterIdentity comparisons for
  peer lookup and election; elect() currently ignores case, while full_id()
  does not. Avoid composite keys built by simple string concatenation; handle
  identical local/remote identities and duplicate peer configurations.
  Files: `include/diameter/core/peer/peer.h`, Manager, Config.

### Controllers, identifiers, and recovery

- [ ] Complete WatchdogController and attach instances to connections:
  INITIAL/OKAY/SUSPECT/DOWN/REOPEN states, pending DWR tracking, traffic events,
  the Tw timer, recovery, failover/failback callbacks, and DWR generation.
  on_generate_DWR_cb is currently registered but never called, and
  watchdog_timeout is unused. Verify the algorithm against RFC 3539 §3.4
  and Appendix A, including jitter and the DWA count required to leave REOPEN.
  Files: WatchdogController, Manager, Peer.

- [ ] Implement reconnect using reconnect_timeout after connection failure
  or loss of an open connection; distinguish connect, CER/CEA, request,
  watchdog, and close timeouts. Do not route all control request timeouts to
  the same Peer::timeout(): OPEN, for example, has no such transition.
  Checks: late answers, prolonged remote node unavailability, and stopping
  reconnect attempts on explicit shutdown. Files: Manager, controllers, I/O.

- [ ] Fix incoming connection ownership in IncomingController: remove waiting
  entries on handoff to Peer/rejection/disconnection, hand off exactly once,
  check callback availability, and do not rely on the later callback of
  a cancelled timer to remove an entry. Protect conn_id generation across
  controller instances. Check back-to-back CER and subsequent messages,
  a timeout concurrent with CER, and controller shutdown.
  File: `include/diameter/core/controller/incoming_controller.h`.

- [ ] Complete request/answer correlation using the specific connection and
  its generation, Command-Code, Application-Id, and R-bit. The current key is
  a peer name and an ID pair; an answer for another command with the same IDs
  can remove a pending request. Handle duplicates, unknown/late answers, and
  push_request() failures, which Manager currently ignores.
  Files: MessageController, Manager.

- [ ] Improve IdsGenerator: replace reliance on unseeded global rand(), check
  Hop-by-Hop collisions among pending requests, handle wraparound, and ensure
  End-to-End uniqueness across restarts. Separately define a Session-Id
  generator in the layer that will own sessions.
  File: `include/diameter/core/ids_generator.h`.

- [ ] Add duplicate request detection and agree with the calling application
  on response storage/replay without repeating side effects. For failover,
  expose pending requests and a callback for selecting an alternate peer,
  and define rules for preserving/replacing IDs and the T flag.
  handle_peer_disconnect() currently only completes requests with NetworkError.
  Files: MessageController, Manager; document the ownership boundary in the API.

### Config, I/O, and concurrency

- [ ] Validate the entire Config before changing a running Manager:
  local_peer_name references, empty host lists (currently accessed via host[0]),
  addresses/ports, roles, supported transports, identities, durations, and
  capabilities. Return a clear error instead of partially applying configuration
  and failing in at(). Include Addr::type in operator==. Files: Config, Manager.

- [ ] Apply updated acceptor/peer/connector configurations, including changes
  to role and local peer; make repeated configure() calls with the same input
  idempotent. Resolve m_connectors ownership: Manager iterates this map, but
  create_peer() never inserts into it. Assign Connector a single owner and
  define object creation/shutdown order during reconfiguration. File: Manager.

- [ ] Implement allow_dynamic_peers and an admission/rejection callback, or
  explicitly reject this unsupported setting. The flag is currently unused.
  Define the unknown-peer policy: closing without CEA is allowed; if choosing
  to answer, return UNKNOWN_PEER. Remove dynamic entries with their connection.
  Files: Config, Manager, IncomingController.

- [ ] Serialize socket/resolver/timer operations and related callbacks through
  a strand/executor, or explicitly restrict the API to one thread. Atomic flags
  and a queue mutex are insufficient for concurrent stop/send/read/connect;
  check the lifetime of the active async_write buffer when clearing the queue.
  Define callback ordering and how user exceptions are handled.
  Files: `include/diameter/core/io/*.h`, controllers, Manager, Peer.

- [ ] Complete Acceptor::stop(): cancel the resolver and retry timer, discard
  completions from a previous run() after restarting, and report
  resolve/open/bind/listen errors to Manager in addition to accept errors.
  Files: `include/diameter/core/io/acceptor.h`, Manager.

- [ ] Complete address list and IPv6 support: currently only host[0] is used,
  and add_host_ip_address(string) always creates an IPv4 Address. When iterating
  endpoints, restore the socket and local bind after an error, and account for
  mismatched local/remote address families. Checks: IPv4/IPv6, multiple addresses,
  and failure of the first endpoint. Files: I/O,
  `application/common/message_builder.h`, `message/avp/value/address.h`.

- [ ] Limit incoming Message size, header/body read time, the number of
  connections waiting for CER, pending requests, and queued outgoing bytes.
  Add notifications for rejection/queue overflow and write failures. Queues
  are currently unbounded, and send_message() silently drops messages on closed
  connections and does not check nullptr. Files: Connection, controllers, Config.

- [ ] Distinguish graceful shutdown from aborting the transport after stream
  corruption; decoding errors currently use ordinary shutdown/close. Verify
  the required behavior against RFC §2.1.
  File: `include/diameter/core/io/connection.h`.

- [ ] Implement TLS/TCP: certificate/trust configuration, remote peer identity
  validation, handshake and its timeout before capabilities exchange, failure
  handling, and proper shutdown. Do not advertise TLS via Inband-Security-Id
  without a working implementation; make legacy inband negotiation an explicit
  compatibility mode. Files: Config, I/O, Manager.

### AVP values

- [ ] Improve DiameterURI: safe port range parsing instead of atoi(), invalid
  transport/protocol combinations, case handling, aaa/aaas defaults, and clearly
  defined getter state before/after validate(). Improve shared DiameterIdentity
  validation and define where it is required on receipt. Active tests now expose
  acceptance of port 65536 and UDP for explicit/default Diameter.
  Files: `include/diameter/message/avp/value/{diameter_uri,diameter_identity}.h`.

- [ ] Accept valid IDNA ASCII A-labels throughout DiameterIdentity and DiameterURI,
  including a final label such as xn--p1ai. Both validators currently reject it
  because their final-label regex allows only letters. Retain rejection of
  raw non-ASCII identities. Regression tests are listed above.
  Files: `include/diameter/message/avp/value/{diameter_identity,diameter_uri}.h`.

- [ ] Reject embedded NUL characters in textual IPv4/IPv6 Address construction.
  inet_pton() currently sees only the prefix, so a std::string containing
  "127.0.0.1\0invalid" is accepted. Validate the complete input string;
  preserve support for zero octets in binary Address/OctetString payloads.
  File: `include/diameter/message/avp/value/address.h`.

- [ ] Replace partial IPFilterRule regex validation with parsing of the supported
  grammar: numeric protocol, addresses and masks, port ranges/lists, negation,
  and options; preserve all parsed fields. Currently, invalid IPs/masks are
  accepted, valid forms are unsupported, and ports are not extracted.
  File: `include/diameter/message/avp/value/ip_filter_rule.h`.

- [ ] Validate derived AVP lengths/formats during semantic parsing:
  IPv4/IPv6 Address, UTF8String, Enumerated, and Time. For Time, add wire
  roundtrip checks at the 2036 boundary and around 2104: existing future-date
  tests mostly check an object with a retained era rather than reconstructing
  it from four bytes. New tests cover inferred 2036 rollover and explicit-era
  2104 conversion; implicit reconstruction at the half-era boundary still needs
  a documented policy. Fix rejection of representable pre-1970 timestamps in
  TimeBase's Unix/NTP conversions, now covered by an active failing test.
  Files: `message/avp/value/*`, `serial/avp/avp.h` under
  include/diameter.

## P2 — new features and responsibility boundaries

- [ ] Document supported library roles and the scope of RFC compliance:
  client/server, relay/proxy/redirect; for §6 and §8–9 requirements, define
  the responsibilities of core, application modules, and calling code. Keep
  business logic and route selection outside core unless needed; provide
  callbacks and verify end-to-end protocol compliance across this boundary.

- [ ] Implement SCTP transport and DTLS/SCTP for the full claimed RFC scope,
  or document the limitations of the current TCP profile. Account for
  multihoming, message boundaries and streams, PPID, and Host-IP-Address for
  node addresses; check interaction with Peer and the watchdog algorithm.

- [ ] Add an extensible application layer: identifiers, AVP dictionaries,
  builders/parsers, and command validators under application/<name>.
  `include/diameter/application/application.h` is currently empty. Prioritize
  specific applications based on library consumers; do not claim application
  support solely because its numeric ID is defined.

- [ ] Complete APIs for base session/accounting commands: RAR/RAA, STR/STA,
  ASR/ASA, and ACR/ACA. Currently, only command/AVP codes exist. Align session
  FSM, timer, and accounting record storage implementation with the
  core/application boundary; separately document stateless operation and
  the responsibilities of the calling application.

- [ ] For required agent roles, add a host/realm/application routing interface,
  Route-Record/Proxy-Info, loop detection, redirect handling, and reverse answer
  correlation. Define ownership of the routing table/cache; these features
  are currently absent and are not needed by every library consumer.

- [ ] If dynamic discovery is needed, add DNS NAPTR/SRV, TTL handling, and
  a peer authorization callback, or define an external discovery API. Keep
  discovery separate from A/AAAA resolution of a configured host in Connector.

## Build, tests, integration, and documentation

- [ ] Fix or explicitly limit DIAMETER_HEADER_ONLY: definitions of
  make_peer_info() and the Peer and WatchdogController tables are in .cpp files.
  Check: tests and both examples build and link in this mode, including use
  from multiple translation units without ODR violations.

- [ ] Fix CMake usage requirements: the STATIC target should propagate
  Netpacker and Boost dependencies through target_link_libraries(PUBLIC),
  and C++17 requirements through target_compile_features(PUBLIC). STATIC link
  dependencies are currently commented out, and the language standard is set
  only on the target itself. Check add_subdirectory() and DIAMETER_USE_SUBMODULES,
  including dependencies outside system include paths and use in a parent project.

- [ ] Add install/export rules and a package configuration for
  find_package(diameter): examples/first already refers to this usage, but
  no package is generated. Check a separate minimal consumer that needs only
  target_link_libraries(... diameter::diameter).

- [ ] Align build options/documentation with actual behavior: README suggests
  cloning Netpacker, but DIAMETER_USE_SUBMODULES defaults to OFF; benchmarks
  are added only inside tests; external dependencies are not pinned.
  Include public headers in documentation: DOXYGEN_INPUT currently covers
  only src.

- [x] Fill the 5 empty Serial tests: Grouped decoding and
  DiameterIdentity/DiameterURI/Enumerated/IPFilterRule encoding.

- [ ] Fill the 2 remaining empty tests (Peer and Manager); extend FSM tests
  from a single transition check to the scenarios above. Add the currently
  missing controller tests, using controllable timers without long sleeps.

- [ ] Add loopback integration tests: two Manager instances, simultaneous
  connection attempts, CER/CEA, application request/answer, watchdog, DPR/DPA,
  reconnect, reconfiguration, and shutdown. Check fragmented TCP input and
  consecutive messages, a multithreaded io_context, and closure during a write.

- [ ] Add negative tests and fuzzing for Message/AVP/Grouped, ASan/UBSan,
  and TSan for supported I/O scenarios. Retain regression tests for the four
  reproduced defects and identified handshake errors. Add STATIC/header-only
  builds and tests to CI, and measure core coverage.

- [ ] Check public header independence from include order and the correctness
  of rarely used APIs. For example, BasicValue::get() const returns a non-const
  pointer; some headers obtain standard types only through transitive includes.
  File: `include/diameter/message/avp/avp.h`.

- [ ] Define how to provide a custom logger_impl.h for STATIC and header-only
  builds. include/diameter/log/logger_impl.h currently contains a std::cout
  implementation: replacing the header only in the application does not change
  an already compiled library. Add an integration example, log levels/disabling,
  and a defined calling-thread contract.

- [ ] Review obsolete/future interfaces based on actual usage:
  `include/diameter/core/connection/connection.h`, IPeer, make_connection_id()
  in Manager, empty source files, and unused aliases. Remove or complete them
  only after determining their intended purpose; update docs diagrams to match
  the resulting API.

- [ ] Update README and examples: a minimal two-node exchange without
  developer-specific addresses, sending/receiving through callbacks, errors
  and timeouts, configuration and its limitations, threading/ownership,
  graceful shutdown, and custom logging. Clarify that docs/configuration.yml
  is a format draft; the library has no YAML loader yet. Decide whether a loader
  belongs here or whether the calling application should populate Config.

- [ ] Check interoperability with an independent Diameter implementation and
  create a mapping of RFC requirement -> implementation/owner -> test -> limitation.
  Until then, describe the current implementation as incomplete.

## Normative references

- [RFC 6733](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6733.html): §2.1–2.2 transport
  and security, §3–4 wire format/AVPs, §5.3–5.6 capabilities and peer FSM,
  §6–7 processing/errors, §8–9 sessions/accounting, §13 security.
- [RFC 3539](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3539.html): §3.4 and Appendix A
  for watchdog implementation. The docs diagram does not replace verification
  against the RFC algorithm.
